## Proposed Title

**Proximity-Actuated Network Architecture for Policy-Controlled Remote Device Operation Using Contextually Overloaded Beacon Payloads**

## Abstract

A proximity-actuated network architecture includes one or more sensor-equipped radiobeacons, a mobile computing device, a policy service, and one or more remotely controllable devices. A radiobeacon obtains sensor data and transmits a short-range wireless payload containing an overloaded bit field whose interpretation depends on contextual information available to the mobile computing device or the policy service. The mobile computing device detects the payload within a wireless proximity region, determines or supplies the contextual information, and communicates a corresponding event message to the policy service through a wide-area network. The policy service evaluates the event message according to an operator-defined policy and, when policy conditions are satisfied, causes a command to be transmitted to a remotely controllable device. Contextual overloading permits a limited-length beacon payload to represent different identifiers, sensor values, event codes, or command-related information in different operational contexts.

## Technical Field

The present disclosure relates generally to wireless networked control systems and, more particularly, to proximity-actuated systems in which sensor-equipped radiobeacons communicate compressed payloads to mobile devices for policy-based control of remote devices.

## Background

Short-range radiobeacons can announce presence, identity, and sensed conditions to nearby mobile devices. Beacon communication protocols, however, may limit the number of bits available in an advertising packet or other connectionless transmission. Conventional systems may dedicate fixed fields to respective data types, thereby limiting the amount or variety of information conveyed in each payload.

Additionally, beacon-triggered systems often associate a detected beacon directly with a predetermined action. Such arrangements provide limited support for operator-specific policies, contextual interpretation, authorization, temporal conditions, sensor-dependent actions, and control of devices located remotely from the beacon or mobile device.

Accordingly, a need exists for an architecture that efficiently represents information in constrained beacon payloads, applies centrally managed operator policies, and selectively controls remote devices based on beacon proximity, sensor state, mobile-device context, or combinations thereof.

## Summary

In an embodiment, a sensor-equipped radiobeacon generates a wireless payload including a context indicator and an overloaded bit field. The overloaded bit field is assigned different semantic meanings under different interpretation contexts. For example, the same bit positions may represent a beacon identifier in a first context, a sensor measurement in a second context, an event code in a third context, or an index into a remotely stored data structure in a fourth context.

A smartphone or other mobile computing device receives the payload while located within communication range of the radiobeacon. The mobile computing device determines an interpretation context based on one or more of the context indicator, a beacon identity, an application state, a geographic location, a time, a stored decoding profile, a user identity, a sensor state, or information obtained from a network service. The mobile computing device may decode the overloaded bit field locally or may forward the bit field and contextual information to a cloud-based policy service.

The policy service applies one or more operator-defined policies to a proximity event derived from the beacon payload. The policies may include identity, authorization, location, time, sensor, rate, sequence, security, and device-state conditions. Upon satisfaction of applicable conditions, the policy service generates or selects a command and causes the command to be delivered to a remote device through a device gateway, message broker, application programming interface, or other communication channel.

## Example Detailed Description

### System Architecture

A system may include:

1. one or more sensor-equipped radiobeacons;
2. one or more smartphones or other mobile computing devices;
3. a wide-area communication network;
4. a cloud-based policy service;
5. an operator interface;
6. a command delivery service; and
7. one or more remotely controllable devices.

Each radiobeacon may include a processor, memory, a short-range wireless transceiver, and at least one sensor. The sensor may include a temperature sensor, motion sensor, accelerometer, proximity sensor, light sensor, humidity sensor, pressure sensor, acoustic sensor, magnetic sensor, chemical sensor, biometric sensor, switch-state sensor, or any combination thereof.

The radiobeacon may communicate using Bluetooth Low Energy, Bluetooth, ultra-wideband, Wi-Fi, near-field communication, IEEE 802.15.4, or another wireless protocol. A payload may be transmitted as an advertising packet, periodic advertisement, response packet, connection-oriented message, or other radio transmission.

### Bit Overloading

As used herein, an “overloaded bit field” is a set of bit positions having a first semantic assignment under a first interpretation context and a different semantic assignment under a second interpretation context. The respective assignments may differ in data type, field boundary, scale, encoding, or source.

By way of example, a sixteen-bit field may represent:

- a device identifier when a mode value is zero;
- a temperature and battery state when the mode value is one;
- an event code and sequence number when the mode value is two; or
- an index referencing a cloud-stored record when the mode value is three.

The interpretation context may be identified explicitly by one or more mode bits or may be inferred from information external to the overloaded bit field. Such external information may include a beacon address, transmission channel, payload type, encryption key, time window, beacon location, smartphone location, user account, application state, previously received payload, sensor threshold state, or operator configuration.

Bit overloading may additionally include temporal overloading, in which successive transmissions assign different meanings to the same bit positions according to a transmission schedule or sequence state. The mobile device or policy service may reconstruct a larger logical message from multiple payloads. In some embodiments, payload compression includes dictionary indexing, delta encoding, quantization, variable-length encoding, field omission, or probabilistic reconstruction in addition to bit overloading.

### Policy Processing and Command Delivery

Upon receiving a beacon payload, the smartphone may generate a proximity event message including some or all of:

- the received payload;
- a decoded payload value;
- a beacon identifier;
- a mobile-device identifier or user token;
- received signal strength;
- estimated distance;
- mobile-device location;
- a timestamp;
- an application state;
- a nonce or sequence value; and
- authentication information.

The policy service may select an operator policy based on the beacon, user, organization, location, remote device, or event type. A policy may require that:

- the smartphone be within a threshold proximity of the beacon;
- a sensed value satisfy a threshold;
- the user possess a required authorization;
- the event occur during an allowed time interval;
- a remote device be in a specified state;
- a required sequence of beacon events have occurred;
- a rate limit not be exceeded; or
- a cryptographic verification be successful.

When the policy conditions are satisfied, the policy service may generate a command for a remote device. The command may actuate a lock, door, light, appliance, industrial controller, alarm, camera, display, vehicle subsystem, HVAC component, medical device, access-control component, or other network-addressable device.

The remote device need not be co-located with the radiobeacon or smartphone. For example, detection of a beacon at a first site may cause operation of a device at a geographically separated second site.

The policy service may receive an acknowledgement or state report from the remote device and may provide a result notification to the smartphone, beacon operator, or audit service.

## Claims

### 1. Independent System Claim

1. **A proximity-actuated network system comprising:**

   a sensor-equipped radiobeacon comprising a sensor and a short-range wireless transmitter, the sensor-equipped radiobeacon being configured to:  
   obtain sensor information from the sensor;  
   generate a wireless payload comprising an overloaded bit field, wherein a common set of bit positions of the overloaded bit field represents a first type of information under a first interpretation context and a different, second type of information under a second interpretation context; and  
   transmit the wireless payload within a wireless proximity region;

   a mobile computing device configured to:  
   receive the wireless payload while the mobile computing device is within the wireless proximity region;  
   obtain contextual information usable to select between at least the first interpretation context and the second interpretation context; and  
   transmit, through a network, an event message based on the wireless payload and the contextual information;

   a network-accessible policy service configured to:  
   receive the event message;  
   identify an operator-defined policy applicable to the event message;  
   evaluate a condition of the operator-defined policy using information represented by the overloaded bit field; and  
   in response to satisfaction of the condition, cause transmission of a device command; and

   a remote device configured to receive the device command and alter an operational state in accordance with the device command.

2. The system of claim 1, wherein the first type of information comprises an identifier and the second type of information comprises a sensor value.

3. The system of claim 1, wherein the overloaded bit field represents an index into a data structure maintained by the network-accessible policy service under at least one of the interpretation contexts.

4. The system of claim 1, wherein the contextual information comprises at least one of a mode indicator, an identity of the sensor-equipped radiobeacon, a geographic location, a time value, an application state, a user identity, a previously received payload, or a sensor state.

5. The system of claim 1, wherein the contextual information is omitted from the common set of bit positions and is inferred by the mobile computing device or the network-accessible policy service.

6. The system of claim 1, wherein the sensor-equipped radiobeacon assigns different semantic meanings to the common set of bit positions during different transmission intervals.

7. The system of claim 6, wherein the mobile computing device or the network-accessible policy service combines values received during a plurality of the transmission intervals to reconstruct a logical message larger than the wireless payload.

8. The system of claim 1, wherein the event message further includes a measure of received signal strength or an estimated distance between the mobile computing device and the sensor-equipped radiobeacon.

9. The system of claim 8, wherein the condition requires the measure of received signal strength or the estimated distance to satisfy a proximity threshold.

10. The system of claim 1, wherein the operator-defined policy further specifies at least one of a user authorization condition, a time condition, a location condition, a sensor threshold, a remote-device state, an event-sequence condition, or a rate limit.

11. The system of claim 1, wherein the remote device is geographically separated from both the sensor-equipped radiobeacon and the mobile computing device.

12. The system of claim 1, wherein the policy service causes transmission of the device command through a message broker, a device gateway, or an application programming interface.

13. The system of claim 1, wherein the wireless payload further comprises a cryptographic authentication value, and the policy service causes transmission of the device command only after verifying the cryptographic authentication value.

14. The system of claim 1, wherein the sensor comprises at least one of a motion sensor, temperature sensor, light sensor, humidity sensor, pressure sensor, magnetic sensor, acoustic sensor, biometric sensor, or switch-state sensor.

### 15. Independent Method Claim

15. **A computer-implemented method comprising:**

   receiving, by a mobile computing device from a sensor-equipped radiobeacon, a short-range wireless payload while the mobile computing device is proximate to the sensor-equipped radiobeacon, the short-range wireless payload comprising an overloaded bit field having a plurality of bit positions;

   determining contextual information associated with the short-range wireless payload;

   selecting, based on the contextual information, one of a plurality of semantic assignments for the plurality of bit positions, a first semantic assignment representing a first data type and a second semantic assignment representing a different, second data type;

   generating a proximity event based at least in part on information obtained using the selected semantic assignment;

   transmitting the proximity event to a cloud-based policy service;

   evaluating, by the cloud-based policy service, the proximity event according to an operator-defined policy; and

   in response to determining that the proximity event satisfies the operator-defined policy, causing a command to be transmitted to a remotely controllable device.

16. The method of claim 15, wherein determining the contextual information comprises obtaining an interpretation profile associated with an identifier of the sensor-equipped radiobeacon.

17. The method of claim 15, wherein selecting one of the plurality of semantic assignments is performed by the cloud-based policy service after the mobile computing device transmits the overloaded bit field without fully decoding the overloaded bit field.

18. The method of claim 15, further comprising validating freshness of the short-range wireless payload using a sequence value, nonce, rolling code, or timestamp.

19. The method of claim 15, further comprising receiving an acknowledgement indicating execution of the command by the remotely controllable device and recording the acknowledgement in an audit record associated with the proximity event.

20. The method of claim 15, wherein the command controls an access-control device, industrial machine, lighting device, alarm device, appliance, vehicle subsystem, or environmental-control device.

### 21. Independent Radiobeacon Claim

21. **A sensor-equipped radiobeacon comprising:**

   at least one sensor;

   a wireless transmitter;

   a memory storing a plurality of payload interpretation configurations; and

   a processor configured to:  
   obtain a sensor value from the at least one sensor;  
   select an interpretation configuration based on an operating context;  
   encode, in accordance with the selected interpretation configuration, information into an overloaded bit field, wherein at least a portion of the overloaded bit field encodes an identifier under a first operating context and sensor-derived information under a second operating context; and  
   cause the wireless transmitter to transmit a connectionless wireless message containing the overloaded bit field for receipt by a mobile computing device.

22. The sensor-equipped radiobeacon of claim 21, wherein the processor is configured to select the interpretation configuration according to a transmission schedule.

23. The sensor-equipped radiobeacon of claim 21, wherein the connectionless wireless message comprises a Bluetooth Low Energy advertising message.

24. The sensor-equipped radiobeacon of claim 21, wherein the processor is further configured to quantize or delta-encode the sensor value before encoding the sensor-derived information.

### 25. Independent Computer-Readable-Medium Claim

25. **A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors of a network-accessible policy system, cause the network-accessible policy system to:**

   receive an event message generated in response to a mobile device detecting a wireless payload transmitted by a sensor-equipped radiobeacon;

   obtain an overloaded bit field from the event message;

   determine an interpretation context for the overloaded bit field using contextual information associated with at least one of the sensor-equipped radiobeacon, the mobile device, a user, a location, or a time;

   decode the overloaded bit field according to the interpretation context;

   select an operator-defined policy based on the decoded overloaded bit field;

   determine that one or more conditions of the operator-defined policy are satisfied; and

   cause a remote device to execute a command associated with the operator-defined policy.

26. The non-transitory computer-readable medium of claim 25, wherein the instructions further cause the network-accessible policy system to reject the event message in response to determining that the event message fails an authentication, freshness, proximity, or authorization condition.

27. The non-transitory computer-readable medium of claim 25, wherein the instructions further cause the network-accessible policy system to select the command based jointly on a sensor value decoded from the overloaded bit field and a current state reported by the remote device.

## Optional Functional Variations

The disclosed architecture may also support:

- multiple smartphones jointly confirming a proximity event;
- multiple beacons providing location triangulation;
- edge-based policy evaluation when cloud connectivity is unavailable;
- delayed command execution after subsequent policy confirmation;
- bidirectional feedback from the remote device;
- rotating beacon identifiers for privacy;
- encrypted or signed overloaded fields;
- federated policy services operated by different entities; and
- machine-learned policy conditions based on event history.

The foregoing embodiments are illustrative and are not intended to limit the scope of the claimed subject matter. Features described in connection with one embodiment may be combined with features of another embodiment unless technically incompatible.
